# Refuse to install a package version published less than 7 days ago.
#
# Why: the most common supply-chain attack on npm is a maintainer account takeover
# followed by a malicious patch release. Those get caught and unpublished fast --
# usually within hours -- so the whole risk window is the first days after publish.
# Waiting a week costs nothing on a project that has no reason to run a release the
# day it ships, and removes the window entirely.
#
# What it does in practice: `npm install` and `npm audit fix` skip a fix whose patched
# version is still inside the cooldown and print a `left at a vulnerable version because
# a fix is newer than the release-age cutoff` warning rather than failing. That warning
# is easy to miss in scrollback -- if an advisory looks unfixable, check for it before
# concluding the fix is blocked upstream. Re-run the audit after the cooldown passes.
#
# Do NOT work around it with `npm audit fix --force` or by lowering this number to get a
# green audit. That trades the guard for the exact days it exists to cover. If a fix is
# genuinely urgent, decide that deliberately and record it in memory-security.md.
#
# This lived only in the maintainer's ~/.npmrc until 2026-09-17, so it protected one
# machine while memory-security.md described it as a repository control. It is committed
# here so the claim is true and every clone inherits it. See CONTRIBUTING.md.
min-release-age=7
